---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description?: string;
  pubDate?: string;
}

const { title, description, pubDate } = Astro.props;
---

<Base title={title} description={description} pubDate={pubDate}>
  <p>
    I spent the better part of a year building a homelab before I admitted what
    it actually was: a portfolio.
  </p>

  <p>
    Not a homelab. Not a &ldquo;lab environment.&rdquo; A portfolio — the kind of
    thing that shows I can actually build the stuff I claim to know.
  </p>

  <h2>Why a blog</h2>

  <p>
    Every job description for a privacy engineer or compliance role I want asks
    for &ldquo;experience.&rdquo; I don&rsquo;t have that. What I have is a homelab
    that touches GDPR-adjacent problems — data residency, retention, subject
    access requests — and the ability to document what I built and why.
  </p>

  <p>
    So this is me building in public. The homelab will stay behind a Tailscale
    mesh, but the thinking gets published.
  </p>

  <h2>What this is</h2>

  <p>
    The nth degree is about engineering depth — the kind you get from running
    production infrastructure, even if that production is one person&rsquo;s
    cloud-native homelab. I&rsquo;m interested in:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Compliance as engineering, not paperwork</li>
    <li>Infrastructure that respects the data it handles</li>
    <li>
      Building things that scale to &ldquo;fifty-person SaaS&rdquo; even if they
      never will
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    No list of takeaways. No conclusion that restates the intro. Just posts that
    try to be worth reading.
  </p>

  <h2>First</h2>

  <p>
    If you&rsquo;re here from LinkedIn — hi. Drop me a message if anything here
    is useful or wrong. I want to know both.
  </p>
</Base>